Rechrom compares canonical contributing layer/property/tile input snapshots,
including raster-owner LayerTreeId, ordered nested effect surfaces and output
space. Unchanged child input retains the pass; all changed child input is fully
damaged. No surface-local coordinate rewrite or source-frame cache is used.

Successful root composition is the consumer commit point. The existing
CompositorFrame frame_token carries a successful-build submission sequence,
independent of source_frame_id; a discontinuity conservatively repaints the
whole surface dependency graph. This is analogous to Chromium's active-frame
continuity requirement, not an assertion that Chromium frame_token and
Surface active_frame_index are the same mechanism. A producer belongs to its
SurfaceId lifetime. Invalidation clears backings/history; normal session
recreation constructs fresh CompositorEngine and Display/Renderer owners.

Early preparation and visual damage wake:
  cc/trees/layer_tree_host_impl.cc (DidScrollContent / SetNeedsRedraw)
  cc/trees/proxy_impl.cc (SetNeedsRedrawOnImplThread)
  cc/scheduler/scheduler_state_machine.cc (needs_redraw / did_draw)

The runtime routes genuine active drawable damage separately from future-frame
and swap-ack demand. Rechrom's early preparation split permits one coalesced retry
after a clean attempt, only in the same cycle before its existing draw deadline.
A produced candidate is latched; delayed or duplicate notifications cannot make
an unlimited preparation loop. This adapts cc's redraw signaling to Rechrom's
pure Viz scheduling owner, rather than claiming identical Chromium scheduling
or changing BeginFrame deadlines or swap limits.

